---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import PageFooter from '../components/PageFooter.astro';
import SectionRule from '../components/SectionRule.astro';
import Ornament from '../components/Ornament.astro';
import Medallion from '../components/Medallion.astro';
import Tailpiece from '../components/Tailpiece.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Research program — CIRWEL Research"
  description="The research program behind UNITARES: the hypotheses under test, the experiments that have closed, the results that came back negative, and the questions that remain open and under-powered. Maintained as a record, not a pitch."
>
  <PageHeader currentPath="/research" />

  <main class="px-6 lg:px-12 max-w-screen-xl mx-auto">

    <!-- HERO -->
    <section class="pt-16 pb-20 lg:pt-28 lg:pb-28 grid grid-cols-12 gap-6">
      <div class="col-span-12 lg:col-span-9">
        <p class="label text-oxblood mb-8 reveal reveal-1">— The research program, including its failures</p>
        <h1 class="display text-5xl md:text-7xl lg:text-8xl mb-10 reveal reveal-2">
          What I am testing.<br/>
          What already <span class="italic">failed.</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-lg md:text-xl leading-relaxed text-stone max-w-prose reveal reveal-3">
          This page is the program behind the paper: the questions under test,
          the experiments that have closed, and the results that came back
          negative. It is kept as a record rather than a pitch. An experiment
          that failed stays on the page, with the number that failed it.
        </p>
        <p class="text-base md:text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink max-w-prose reveal reveal-3 mt-6">
          I am one person. There is no group behind the plural, so there is no
          plural: everything below was set, run, and recorded by me, against a
          fleet I operate myself.
        </p>
        <Ornament size="floret" class="mt-14 opacity-70 reveal reveal-3" />
      </div>
    </section>

    <hr class="hairline mb-2"/>

    <!-- RECEIPTS -->
    <section class="py-10 grid grid-cols-12 gap-6 reveal reveal-4">
      <div class="col-span-12 lg:col-span-3 mb-4 lg:mb-0">
        <p class="label text-oxblood">— The receipts</p>
      </div>
      <dl class="col-span-12 lg:col-span-9 grid grid-cols-12 gap-y-5 gap-x-6">

        <dt class="col-span-4 md:col-span-3 label text-stone pt-1">Program</dt>
        <dd class="col-span-8 md:col-span-9">
          Runtime self-state telemetry for AI-agent fleets
          <span class="text-stone">· begun September 2025</span>
        </dd>

        <dt class="col-span-4 md:col-span-3 label text-stone pt-1">Operator</dt>
        <dd class="col-span-8 md:col-span-9">
          <span class="smcp">Kenny Wang</span>
          <span class="text-stone">· sole operator, no staff · ORCID</span>
          <a href="https://orcid.org/0009-0006-7544-2374" class="font-mono text-sm">0009-0006-7544-2374</a>
        </dd>

        <dt class="col-span-4 md:col-span-3 label text-stone pt-1">Evidence base</dt>
        <dd class="col-span-8 md:col-span-9">
          4M+ governance events
          <span class="text-stone">· one operator's fleet, in continuous operation since November 2025.
          Self-traffic, not adoption.</span>
        </dd>

        <dt class="col-span-4 md:col-span-3 label text-stone pt-1">Papers</dt>
        <dd class="col-span-8 md:col-span-9 leading-relaxed">
          <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.19647159" class="font-mono text-sm">10.5281/zenodo.19647159</a>
          <span class="text-stone"> · UNITARES, CC-BY 4.0</span><br/>
          <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20098168" class="font-mono text-sm">10.5281/zenodo.20098168</a>
          <span class="text-stone"> · Trajectory Identity, preprint</span><br/>
          <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.19705151" class="font-mono text-sm">10.5281/zenodo.19705151</a>
          <span class="text-stone"> · counterfactual replay data</span>
        </dd>

        <dt class="col-span-4 md:col-span-3 label text-stone pt-1">Reproduction</dt>
        <dd class="col-span-8 md:col-span-9 font-mono text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <a href="https://github.com/cirwel/unitares-repro-v6">github.com/cirwel/unitares-repro-v6</a>
          <span class="text-stone"> · repro kit</span><br/>
          <a href="https://huggingface.co/hikewa">huggingface.co/hikewa</a>
          <span class="text-stone"> · datasets &amp; distilled models</span>
        </dd>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <SectionRule class="mt-2 mb-20" />

    <!-- §01 THE QUESTION -->
    <section id="question" class="py-16 lg:py-24 grid grid-cols-12 gap-6">
      <div class="col-span-12 lg:col-span-3 mb-6 lg:mb-0">
        <p class="label text-oxblood">§01 — The question under test</p>
        <Medallion roman="I" class="hidden lg:block mt-7" />
      </div>
      <div class="col-span-12 lg:col-span-9">
        <h2 class="display text-3xl md:text-5xl mb-8 leading-tight max-w-prose">
          Does an agent's own state carry information its outputs do not?
        </h2>
        <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-stone max-w-prose mb-8">
          UNITARES measures each agent continuously while it works and grades it
          against its own operating point rather than one fleet-wide formula.
          The program asks whether that reading is worth taking. It separates
          into three questions that succeed or fail independently, and conflating
          them is the most common way this kind of claim goes wrong.
        </p>

        <dl class="max-w-prose space-y-6">
          <div>
            <dt class="display text-xl mb-2">1. Discrimination</dt>
            <dd class="text-stone leading-relaxed">
              Are agents distinguishable from each other by state alone, and does
              a given agent stay distinguishable over time?
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class="display text-xl mb-2">2. Prediction</dt>
            <dd class="text-stone leading-relaxed">
              Does prior state anticipate a bad outcome better than simply knowing
              the previous outcome? This is the load-bearing one, and it is the
              one with a negative result.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class="display text-xl mb-2">3. Intervention</dt>
            <dd class="text-stone leading-relaxed">
              Does acting on the state estimate improve anything measurable? Not
              yet answerable at this fleet size, and I say so rather than
              gesturing at it.
            </dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </div>
    </section>

    <SectionRule class="my-8" />

    <!-- §02 SETTLED -->
    <section id="settled" class="py-16 lg:py-24 grid grid-cols-12 gap-6">
      <div class="col-span-12 lg:col-span-3 mb-6 lg:mb-0">
        <p class="label text-oxblood">§02 — Closed, including the negatives</p>
        <Medallion roman="II" class="hidden lg:block mt-7" />
      </div>
      <div class="col-span-12 lg:col-span-9">
        <h2 class="display text-3xl md:text-5xl mb-8 leading-tight max-w-prose">
          Two results. One of them is a failure.
        </h2>

        <div class="max-w-prose space-y-10">
          <div>
            <p class="label text-stone mb-3">Result · calibration changes the reading</p>
            <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">
              In offline counterfactual replay over a 13,310-row production slice,
              <strong>28.9% of risk-band assignments would change</strong> when each
              agent class is measured against its own operating point instead of one
              fleet-wide formula.
            </p>
            <p class="text-stone leading-relaxed">
              Three caveats travel with that number and none of them are optional.
              It is <em>would change</em>, not <em>changed</em>: this is offline replay,
              not a measured benefit of the deployed mechanism. Two things vary at once
              in the comparison (class-conditional grounding and the coherence form), so
              it cannot be attributed to calibration alone. And it is one honest snapshot
              of a moving quantity: the published reproduction CSV yields 28.8% on 13,292
              rows, while the same counterfactual run four days later yields 44.3%.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <p class="label text-oxblood mb-3">Negative result · prediction does not beat persistence</p>
            <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">
              Where outcome prediction was measurable at all, prior state lost.
              <strong>Zero of seven streams beat a last-value persistence baseline.</strong>
            </p>
            <p class="text-stone leading-relaxed mb-4">
              A pre-registered confirmatory read on this question is scheduled, with a
              kill criterion written before the data is seen. Between now and then the
              question is closed to ad-hoc re-runs, because repeatedly asking it surfaces
              a selected maximum that the null model already accounts for.
            </p>
            <p class="text-stone leading-relaxed">
              The measurement was powered to see a moderate effect: at 101 independent
              bad clusters, the minimum detectable lift in ranking quality is around
              0.05. I state that as sensitivity evidence and not as a verdict, because a
              ranking statistic is the wrong instrument to hang a verdict on. It is
              threshold-free, so it averages over operating points no one would deploy;
              it is insensitive at this class imbalance; and it is silent on calibration,
              which is the property a verdict boundary at a fixed threshold actually
              depends on.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <p class="label text-stone mb-3">What would settle it, and has not been run</p>
            <p class="text-stone leading-relaxed">
              Discrimination is not decision value. The question that matters operationally
              is whether acting on the state estimate at the deployed threshold beats not
              acting, and no ranking metric answers that in either direction. Settling it
              needs interventional data or a fleet I do not operate. Until then the honest
              status is that the cheap version of the question has a negative answer and
              the expensive version is untouched.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <p class="label text-stone mb-3">Method finding · the fleet cannot answer this question</p>
            <p class="text-stone leading-relaxed">
              Validating prediction on organic fleet traffic turns out to be structurally
              impossible rather than merely underpowered: the population of tool failures
              and the population carrying state readings are disjoint. This was discovered
              by building the analysis and finding the join empty. It is recorded so the
              next person does not spend the same weeks on it.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <SectionRule class="my-8" />

    <!-- §03 OPEN -->
    <section id="open" class="py-16 lg:py-24 grid grid-cols-12 gap-6">
      <div class="col-span-12 lg:col-span-3 mb-6 lg:mb-0">
        <p class="label text-oxblood">§03 — Open, and under-powered</p>
        <Medallion roman="III" class="hidden lg:block mt-7" />
      </div>
      <div class="col-span-12 lg:col-span-9">
        <h2 class="display text-3xl md:text-5xl mb-8 leading-tight max-w-prose">
          Untested as deployed. Not refuted.
        </h2>
        <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-stone max-w-prose mb-6">
          Agents are distinguishable and their state is non-stationary, but the
          pre-registered self-predictability test did not clear its own bar against
          a persistence and AR(1) null. That test ran on roughly four effective
          agents. Four is not a sample; it is an anecdote with error bars.
        </p>
        <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed max-w-prose mb-6">
          The distinction I hold to, because collapsing it would be its own
          inaccuracy: thin labels and a four-agent fleet are limits of the
          <em>instrument</em>, not verdicts on the idea. Equally, an untested
          hypothesis is not a supported one, and I do not present it as one.
        </p>
        <blockquote class="border-l-2 border-oxblood pl-6 my-10 max-w-prose">
          <p class="display text-xl md:text-2xl leading-snug italic">
            Some of what I set out to demonstrate, I cannot yet demonstrate.
          </p>
        </blockquote>
        <p class="text-stone leading-relaxed max-w-prose">
          What closes this gap is not more analysis of the same four agents. It is
          either a fleet I do not operate, or an adversarial evaluation with labels
          I did not generate. Both require someone other than me.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <SectionRule class="my-8" />

    <!-- §04 METHOD -->
    <section id="method" class="py-16 lg:py-24 grid grid-cols-12 gap-6">
      <div class="col-span-12 lg:col-span-3 mb-6 lg:mb-0">
        <p class="label text-oxblood">§04 — How the program is run</p>
        <Medallion roman="IV" class="hidden lg:block mt-7" />
      </div>
      <div class="col-span-12 lg:col-span-9">
        <h2 class="display text-3xl md:text-5xl mb-8 leading-tight max-w-prose">
          Commitments that cost something.
        </h2>
        <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-stone max-w-prose mb-10">
          A one-person program has no peer review and no colleague to catch a
          convenient reading. These are the substitutes. They are only worth
          stating because each one has already cost me a result I wanted.
        </p>

        <dl class="max-w-prose space-y-8">
          <div>
            <dt class="display text-xl mb-2">Pre-registration with a kill criterion</dt>
            <dd class="text-stone leading-relaxed">
              Hypotheses get a fixed read date and a written criterion for
              abandonment, both set before the data is seen. One has already fired
              and returned a failure. The criterion executed as written.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class="display text-xl mb-2">No ad-hoc re-runs of a bounded question</dt>
            <dd class="text-stone leading-relaxed">
              Once a question has a measured bound, it gets one scheduled read.
              Re-asking until the answer improves is the most comfortable way to
              fool yourself with real data.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class="display text-xl mb-2">A verified-claims registry, with retirements</dt>
            <dd class="text-stone leading-relaxed">
              Every figure appearing in a paper, grant, or on this site resolves to
              one source of record with its caveat attached. Figures found to be
              wrong go in a retired table and are never used again. Two of them were
              dataset row counts I had misread as download counts.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class="display text-xl mb-2">Stated scope of enforcement</dt>
            <dd class="text-stone leading-relaxed">
              A paused agent is blocked from further check-ins and knowledge-graph
              writes. It is not a sandbox and it does not block file writes. The
              stronger claim would be easier to make and would not survive contact.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class="display text-xl mb-2">Single-operator disclosure</dt>
            <dd class="text-stone leading-relaxed">
              Every number here comes from a fleet I run myself. Event volume is
              self-traffic, not adoption. Stating this is what makes the rest of the
              numbers worth reading.
            </dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </div>
    </section>

    <SectionRule class="my-8" />

    <!-- §05 WANTED -->
    <section id="wanted" class="py-16 lg:py-24 grid grid-cols-12 gap-6">
      <div class="col-span-12 lg:col-span-3 mb-6 lg:mb-0">
        <p class="label text-oxblood">§05 — What the program needs</p>
        <Medallion roman="V" class="hidden lg:block mt-7" />
      </div>
      <div class="col-span-12 lg:col-span-9">
        <h2 class="display text-3xl md:text-5xl mb-8 leading-tight max-w-prose">
          A skeptic, not an endorsement.
        </h2>
        <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-stone max-w-prose mb-6">
          The binding constraint on this program is not compute, funding, or code.
          It is that every result so far has been produced and checked by the same
          person. The single most useful thing anyone can do with it is try to break
          it on data I did not generate.
        </p>
        <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed max-w-prose mb-10">
          Concretely: an adversarial evaluation on a public agentic-misalignment
          benchmark, run by someone with no stake in the answer, against a trivial
          baseline. If the state reading adds nothing over the baseline, that is a
          publishable result and I will publish it.
        </p>
        <p class="text-stone leading-relaxed max-w-prose">
          If you work on evaluations, multi-agent systems, or runtime oversight and
          want to take a swing at it, the code and data are open and the reproduction
          kit runs.
          <a href="/#contact" class="font-medium">Get in touch</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="flex justify-center pt-10 pb-4">
      <Tailpiece class="opacity-80" />
    </div>
  </main>

  <PageFooter />
</BaseLayout>
